      "exact_html": "<p id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\"><span id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1\" class=\"ltx_text ltx_font_italic\">At <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\kappa=2\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the spectrum in\nEq. (<a href=\"#S4.E11\" title=\"In 4.1 The Hard Family ‣ 4 Lower Bound via Hidden Eigenbasis ‣ On Complexity of Gaussian Sampling\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">11</span></a>) recovers the original construction in <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib3\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">22</a>]</cite>.\nMore generally, if <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"A_{2}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the original precision matrix\nwith the same rotation <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"O\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">O</annotation></semantics></math>, then\n<math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"A=(\\kappa-1)A_{2}-(\\kappa-2)I_{d}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=(\\kappa-1)A_{2}-(\\kappa-2)I_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus one matrix–vector reply for either matrix determines\nthe corresponding reply for the other.\nThe proof compares <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\sum_{i}\\cos(n\\theta_{i})\\langle x,Oe_{i}\\rangle^{2}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_{i}\\cos(n\\theta_{i})\\langle x,Oe_{i}\\rangle^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\|x\\|^{2}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|x\\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"n=2q+1\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2q+1</annotation></semantics></math>.\nFor the output of a <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"q\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q</annotation></semantics></math>-query algorithm, the ratio is at most <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"d^{-\\Omega(1)}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d^{-\\Omega(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> in absolute value\n(Lemma <a href=\"#S4.Thmtheorem4\" title=\"Lemma 4.4 (Uniform bound on the Krylov space). ‣ A uniform bound on the Krylov space. ‣ 4.2 Properties of the Hard Family ‣ 4 Lower Bound via Hidden Eigenbasis ‣ On Complexity of Gaussian Sampling\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">4.4</span></a>), whereas under the target the ratio of the means has absolute value\n<math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"e^{-\\Theta(n/\\sqrt{\\kappa})}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-\\Theta(n/\\sqrt{\\kappa})}</annotation></semantics></math> (Lemma <a href=\"#S4.Thmtheorem5\" title=\"Lemma 4.5 (Moments under the target). ‣ Target moments. ‣ 4.2 Properties of the Hard Family ‣ 4 Lower Bound via Hidden Eigenbasis ‣ On Complexity of Gaussian Sampling\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">4.5</span></a>).\nHence the test works for <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"n\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math> up to order <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\sqrt{\\kappa}\\log d\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace=\"0.167em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace=\"0.167em\">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\kappa}\\log d</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m13\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\log d\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace=\"0.167em\">⁡</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log d</annotation></semantics></math> as at\n<math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m14\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\kappa=2\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=2</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the source of the factor <math id=\"S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m15\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\sqrt{\\kappa}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><msqrt><mi>κ</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{\\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math> in\nTheorem <a href=\"#S4.Thmtheorem1\" title=\"Theorem 4.1 (Extension to general 𝜅). ‣ 4 Lower Bound via Hidden Eigenbasis ‣ On Complexity of Gaussian Sampling\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">4.1</span></a>.</span></p>",
      "rendered_text": "At κ = 2 \\kappa=2 , the spectrum in\nEq. ( 11 ) recovers the original construction in [ 22 ] .\nMore generally, if A 2 A_{2} denotes the original precision matrix\nwith the same rotation O O , then A = ( κ − 1 ) ​ A 2 − ( κ − 2 ) ​ I d A=(\\kappa-1)A_{2}-(\\kappa-2)I_{d} .\nThus one matrix–vector reply for either matrix determines\nthe corresponding reply for the other.\nThe proof compares ∑ i cos ⁡ ( n ​ θ i ) ​ ⟨ x , O ​ e i ⟩ 2 \\sum_{i}\\cos(n\\theta_{i})\\langle x,Oe_{i}\\rangle^{2} with ‖ x ‖ 2 \\|x\\|^{2} , where n = 2 ​ q + 1 n=2q+1 .\nFor the output of a q q -query algorithm, the ratio is at most d − Ω ⁡ ( 1 ) d^{-\\Omega(1)} in absolute value\n(Lemma 4.4 ), whereas under the target the ratio of the means has absolute value e − Θ ⁡ ( n / κ ) e^{-\\Theta(n/\\sqrt{\\kappa})} (Lemma 4.5 ).\nHence the test works for n n up to order κ ​ log ⁡ d \\sqrt{\\kappa}\\log d , rather than log ⁡ d \\log d as at κ = 2 \\kappa=2 , which is the source of the factor κ \\sqrt{\\kappa} in\nTheorem 4.1 .",
